//@ unimplemented "`Undefined function or type 'printf'` at 18:5, and it is the only diagnostic in the file. Two things this note used to say about it are corrected here, 2026-08-27. It blamed the search path -- \"printf lives in the standard library, which is on no search path (src/driver/SearchPaths.hpp:107)\" -- and that is measurably false: importing.fin:4's `import { printf } from stdio;` resolves against the bundled library today, so printf is findable and simply unbound here. Measured across the corpus, sixteen samples call printf, fourteen declare or import it, and exactly two were bare -- this file and const.fin:68. And this note used to defer to \"the prelude question\", which the owner has since ruled on and split in two. The half this line sits on is answered: a declaration marked `#[global]` is visible to every file in the session with no import, the attribute is usable only inside the `std` namespace, and `printf` is to carry it while nothing else does. So line 18 is expected to resolve with no edit to this sample, and the note records that rather than pre-empting it. A repair was prepared for this file and withdrawn for that reason: it would have added the fourteen-times-verbatim `@define printf(fmt: string, ...) <noret>;` to line 7's blank, and the language is about to make that line redundant. Adding text to the specification that the compiler then makes unnecessary is churn on the specification, which is the thing this corpus is most obliged not to churn. `#[global]` does not exist in the tree yet -- nothing under src/ or lib/ mentions it -- so this stays `unimplemented` and the diagnostic stays correct until it lands. What is behind it is measured rather than guessed: with the declaration supplied in a scratch copy, this file's only remaining diagnostic is `error: codegen: a call to the method 'to_string' on struct 'User' is not lowered yet` at 17:22, so the moment printf resolves this sample leaves the front end for the backend and nothing else here is wrong. The method call on the bounded type parameter `T` at :17 already resolves: a generic parameter's bound is stored on its GenericType and getStructType consults it, so `item.to_string()` finds Printable's method, where this sample reported `Type 'T' does not have methods` before that. Nothing in this file has moved -- it is cited at 4, 6, 8, 12, 16 and 25, and only this note line has ever been rewritten"
// 1. Define an Interface
interface Printable {
    pub fun to_string() <string>;
}

// 2. Define a Struct that "should" implement it (we don't check impl yet, just syntax)
struct User {
    name <string>,
    age <int>
}

// 3. Function with Generic Constraint
// T must implement Printable (checked in Semantics)
fun print_any<T: Printable>(item: T) <noret> { // an easier way to write this is : `fun print_any(item: Printable) <noret> {}`
    let s <string> = item.to_string();
    printf("%s", s);
}

fun main() <noret> {
    let u <User> = User { name: "Fin", age: 1 };
    // This would technically fail in a real compiler if User doesn't impl Printable
    // But for now, we just want to see if Semantics accepts the syntax.
    print_any::<User>(u);
}
